---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import Btu from '@/components/tools/calc2/Btu.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="btu-calculator">
  <Btu client:load />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How the BTU calculator sizes an air conditioner</h2>
    <p>
      The BTU calculator looks up the floor area in the ENERGY STAR table for room air conditioners, then
      adjusts the result for sun, people and cooking, the same way the table's notes do.
    </p>
    <pre><code>base     = ENERGY STAR value for the floor area
sun      = −10 % for a heavily shaded room, +10 % for a very sunny room
people   = +600 BTU/h for each regular occupant over two
kitchen  = +4,000 BTU/h
cooling  = base + sun + people + kitchen</code></pre>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Floor area</th><th>BTU/h</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody class="tabular-nums">
        <tr><td>100 to 150 ft²</td><td>5,000</td></tr>
        <tr><td>150 to 250 ft²</td><td>6,000</td></tr>
        <tr><td>250 to 300 ft²</td><td>7,000</td></tr>
        <tr><td>300 to 350 ft²</td><td>8,000</td></tr>
        <tr><td>350 to 400 ft²</td><td>9,000</td></tr>
        <tr><td>400 to 450 ft²</td><td>10,000</td></tr>
        <tr><td>450 to 550 ft²</td><td>12,000</td></tr>
        <tr><td>550 to 700 ft²</td><td>14,000</td></tr>
        <tr><td>700 to 1,000 ft²</td><td>18,000</td></tr>
        <tr><td>1,000 to 1,200 ft²</td><td>21,000</td></tr>
        <tr><td>1,200 to 1,400 ft²</td><td>23,000</td></tr>
        <tr><td>1,400 to 1,500 ft²</td><td>24,000</td></tr>
        <tr><td>1,500 to 2,000 ft²</td><td>30,000</td></tr>
        <tr><td>2,000 to 2,500 ft²</td><td>34,000</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>Each row includes its upper limit: 400 ft² gives 9,000 BTU/h and 401 ft² gives 10,000 BTU/h.</p>

    <h2>Worked example</h2>
    <p>
      A sunny 20 × 20 foot living room (400 ft²) used by four people starts at 9,000 BTU/h. Sun adds 10 %
      (900), and two people over the first two add 1,200, so the room needs 11,100 BTU/h. The nearest
      common size above that is 12,000 BTU/h, about 3.5 kW of cooling.
    </p>

    <h2>The heating estimate</h2>
    <p>
      For heating, the calculator multiplies the room volume by the temperature rise and an insulation
      factor of 0.133 (good), 0.2 (average) or 0.25 (poor) BTU/h per cubic foot per °F. A 12 × 15 foot room
      with an 8 foot ceiling is 1,440 ft³; with good insulation and a rise of 40 °F it needs about 7,700 BTU/h,
      or 2.2 kW. These factors are rules of thumb.
    </p>

    <h2>Limits</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>The table is for one room cooled by a room air conditioner. Central systems for a whole house need a Manual J load calculation.</li>
      <li>High ceilings, large windows, poor insulation and hot climates all raise the load beyond the table.</li>
      <li>1 kW of cooling is 3,412 BTU/h. The electrical input of a unit is lower; divide by its efficiency ratio (EER or CEER).</li>
    </ul>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
